 is create this idea of artificial  scarcity 24-hour daily deals and even  more put in eight-hour flash sales both  give you a limited time to get the  benefit and remember once those deals  are gone they're gone  so why does this make us more willing to  part with our cash well first off  psychological study has repeatedly shown  that we value things more highly when we  think they are rare scarcity straight-up  enhances desirability being estimating  the value of artwork wine or even  cookies meaning people repeatedly assign  higher value to rarer objects another  reason why these time limiting deals can  seem so compelling is down to something  called loss aversion this is our natural  human tendency to value avoiding a loss  much more highly than potentially  getting it gained imagine losing 20  pounds on the street say our experiments  have shown that it's likely you would  need to find as much as 30 or even 40  pounds at a later date to make up for  the pain of that initial loss all this  boils down to our brains truly hating to  lose out on a good deal so we'd rather  just purchase that game in the 8 hour  flash sale rather than risk feeling the  pain of missing out steam also do a  great job of exploiting gamification now  this is essentially using elements from  games to encourage people to participate  steam trading cards are a great example  these are cards which you earn and  unlock just by playing games on Steam  these can then be crafted into badges  for your profile which are new rewards  and of course kudos from your Steam  friends during a sale however steam  introduces new cards which can be earned  in a number of ways but also through  making game purchases or even buying the  cards directly  they can also be crafted into badges too  which makes for a tempting cherry on top  reason for making that purchase you were  undecided on this whole process exploits  what is known as the endowed Progress  effect now this is when if someone feels  they're making progress towards a goal  they're more likely to be committed to  then finishing that goal the gotta catch  them all effect if you will  as Jamie Madigan points out in his  excellent article the psychology behind  steam summer sale just showing that  you've begun progress towards the goal  is enough to create some mental  ancien over not having yet reached it  and some people are likely to toss in  just one more cheap game to get them  over that hump  in the most recent steam summer sale  valve brought in something called the  steam summer adventure and in doing so  that something very tricky indeed for  the first time they made people join  teams everyone who participated in the  sale was randomly assigned a team blue  pink green purple or red now various  actions such as crafting badges would  earn your team points then at the end of  each day the team with the most points  would win and some members of that team  who had contributed would win prizes  sounds fair enough  the thing is though this exploits our  natural tendency towards belonging to  groups the idea of in groups and air  groups psychology research has  demonstrated time and time again that  when people are put into teams they  naturally behave positively to those in  their team and negatively towards others  even when assigned to a team at random  so if your team red naturally you're  gonna want team red to win and so you  may be more inclined to collect more  cards craft more badges and earn more  points very clever steam very clever but  what other clever things could steam do  in the future to further exploit our  silly brains well in addition to being  our newest video producer Lucy also has  a psychology degree so Lizzie you want  to field this one okay well what about  the idea of reciprocity which is the  fancy psychology way of saying that we  feel the need to return a favor in kind  so what kind of favor could steam do for  us well what about a free game we might  not even play it but th